  ],
  "text": "Nobody wrote the rules for recognising a cat. The system worked them out from examples. Ordinary software is rules a person wrote down. If this, then that. Nobody can write down the rule for a cat. Every rule you try has an exception. So instead you build a board of connections and set every one of them to a random strength. Show it a picture, let it guess, and measure exactly how wrong the guess was. Then nudge every connection slightly in whichever direction would have made it less wrong. Do that a few million times and it holds rules nobody wrote and nobody can read. A language model is the same machine, guessing the next word instead of the animal. It is not retrieving a fact. It is predicting what usually comes next. Prediction at enough scale looks a great deal like understanding, and that argument is not settled. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
